      "text": "1. Given that \\(\\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need to find the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\).\n\n2. First, recall that the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\) is given by \\((\\mathbf{A}^2)^{-1} = (\\mathbf{A}^{-1})^2\\).\n\n3. We need to calculate \\((\\mathbf{A}^{-1})^2\\). This can be done by multiplying \\(\\mathbf{A}^{-1}\\) by itself:\n\n\\[\n(\\mathbf{A}^{-1})^2 = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\n4. Perform the matrix multiplication:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot (-1) & 2 \\cdot 5 + 5 \\cdot (-3) \\\\ -1 \\cdot 2 + (-3) \\cdot (-1) & -1 \\cdot 5 + (-3) \\cdot (-3) \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\n5. Calculate each element of the resulting matrix:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 4 - 5 & 10 - 15 \\\\ -2 + 3 & -5 + 9 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\n6. Therefore, the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\) is:\n\n\\[\n(\\mathbf{A}^2)^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]",
