      "text": "Given \\(\\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need to find the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\).\n\nFirst, recall the property of matrix inverses that states \\((\\mathbf{A}^{-1})^{-1} = \\mathbf{A}\\). Therefore, \\(\\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{I}\\), where \\(\\mathbf{I}\\) is the identity matrix. This means \\(\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\mathbf{I}\\).\n\nWe can use this property to find \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\). Specifically, we have:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{A}\n\\]\n\nTo find \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\), we need to multiply \\(\\mathbf{A}^{-1}\\) by \\(\\mathbf{A}\\). However, we can use the fact that \\(\\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{I}\\) to write:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{A} (\\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\mathbf{A}) = \\mathbf{A} (\\mathbf{I}) = \\mathbf{A}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\((\\mathbf{A}^2)^{-1}\\). Since \\(\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{A}\\), we have:\n\\[\n(\\mathbf{A}^2)^{-1} = (\\mathbf{A})^{-1}\n\\]\n\nWe are given \\(\\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}\\). Therefore, the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\) is:\n\\[\n(\\mathbf{A}^2)^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThus, the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}}\\).",
