      "text": "From the given information, we have $\\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}.$\nWe want to find the inverse of $\\mathbf{A}^2.$\nWe can write $\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{A} \\cdot \\mathbf{A}.$\nThe inverse of a matrix product is the product of the inverses in reverse order, so we have:\n$\\mathbf{A}^2^{-1} = (\\mathbf{A} \\cdot \\mathbf{A})^{-1} = \\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\cdot \\mathbf{A}^{-1}$.\nSubstituting the given value of $\\mathbf{A}^{-1}$, we have:\n$\\mathbf{A}^2^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}$.\nMultiplying the matrices, we get:\n$\\mathbf{A}^2^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot (-1) & 2 \\cdot 5 + 5 \\cdot (-3) \\\\ -1 \\cdot 2 + (-3) \\cdot (-1) & -1 \\cdot 5 + (-3) \\cdot (-3) \\end{pmatrix}$.\nSimplifying, we have:\n$\\mathbf{A}^2^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 - 5 & 10 - 15 \\\\ -2 + 3 & -5 + 9 \\end{pmatrix}$.\nFurther simplifying, we get:\n$\\mathbf{A}^2^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}$.\nTherefore, the inverse of $\\mathbf{A}^2$ is $\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}}$.The answer is: \\begin{pmatrix}-1&-5\\1&4\\end{pmatrix}",
